13. Findings
Finding 1 — Unauthenticated WebDAV File Upload

Severity: Critical

The /test/ endpoint permitted unauthenticated PUT requests, allowing arbitrary files to be written to a web-accessible directory.

Because server-side PHP execution was also available, this could be chained into arbitrary command execution.

Impact:

Arbitrary file upload
Server-side code execution
Initial system compromise
Potential complete host compromise

Remediation:

Disable WebDAV if unnecessary.
Disable unauthenticated PUT requests.
Require authentication and authorization for file-writing operations.
Prevent execution of server-side scripts in upload directories.
Store uploaded content outside the web root.
Apply strict file-type validation.
Finding 2 — Outdated and Vulnerable chkrootkit

Severity: High

The target was running chkrootkit 0.49, which contained a known local privilege-escalation vulnerability.

Because the vulnerable component was executed with elevated privileges, a low-privileged attacker who had already obtained local access could potentially escalate privileges to root.

Impact:

Local privilege escalation
Root-level system compromise

Remediation:

Upgrade chkrootkit to a patched version.
Remove unnecessary security utilities.
Ensure privileged scheduled tasks cannot execute attacker-controlled files.
Apply least-privilege principles to scheduled jobs.
Finding 3 — Obsolete Operating System

Severity: High

The target was running Ubuntu 12.04.4 LTS, which is an obsolete operating system release.

Running unsupported software significantly increases the likelihood of known vulnerabilities remaining exploitable.

Remediation:

Upgrade to a supported Ubuntu LTS release.
Maintain regular security patching.
Remove obsolete packages and services.
